        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "b4640800-7458-53a3-8876-be9b9bf413c5",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Peter Singer, Peter Unger\n- Works: \"Famine, Affluence, and Morality\", Living High and Letting Die\n- Concepts: moral obligation, human suffering, premature death, developed world, third world\n\nFacts:\n- In 1971, Peter Singer wrote the essay \"Famine, Affluence, and Morality.\"\n- Peter Singer was born on 6 July 1946.\n- Peter Singer is an Australian moral philosopher.\n- Living High and Letting Die says that Peter Unger was inspired by Peter Singer's 1971 essay \"Famine, Affluence, and Morality.\"\n- Peter Unger argues in Living High and Letting Die that people in the developed world are morally obliged to make sacrifices to help reduce human suffering and premature death in the third world.\n- Peter Unger further argues in Living High and Letting Die that lying, cheating, and stealing are acceptable and morally right when they mitigate suffering.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "ac853543-f13c-5c38-baa5-e3aa78374962",
        "text": "Page: Living High and Letting Die. Sentence: Inspired by Peter Singer's 1971 essay \"Famine, Affluence, and Morality,\" Unger argues that for people in the developed world to live morally, they are morally obliged to make sacrifices to help mitigate human suffering and premature death in the third world, and further that it is acceptable (and morally right) to lie, cheat, and steal to mitigate suffering.\nPage: Peter Singer. Sentence: Peter Albert David Singer, AC (born 6 July 1946) is an Australian moral philosopher.",
